es than any  president in history he's deported more  illegal immigrants than any president in  history he's held more fundraisers than  any incumbent president running for  reelection a lot of things like that  that might concern progressives more  medical marijuana raids than any  president in history so in that sense he  has a lot to boast about and yet I don't  really hear him boasting about most  kaito just came out of about nine months  of a pretty public and unpleasant battle  between some of the founding sort of  members or influential people about the  governance structure and whatever's  basically edgren versus the koch  brothers it was resolved a few weeks ago  and now john allison from BB&amp;T the bank  and is a great libertarian of the zone  is now the head of it for a couple of  years kato change tell us what we can  think or know about kato in the future  in the wake of this which has now been  set well what happened was charles koch  thought this old document essentially  made him and his brother the owners of  the cato institute they filed suit it  wasn't just add crane it was pretty much  the entire staff and the entire board of  directors who resisted that a settlement  was then reached which involves ed crane  the co-founder and 35 years CEO retiring  somewhat earlier than he would have and  being replaced by john allison that  hasn't happened yet it'll happen in the  fall but it means we dissolve the  shareholder agreement that precipitated  right look crisis so from now on the  board of directors is clearly in control  of the Cato Institute and I think that  means we will stay on the same clear  libertarian keel that we have been John  alison has not yet been directly  involved he hasn't become CEO yet and so  I can't say how's life different what I  can say is he's one of the great  entrepreneurs of the past 20 years he  took BB&amp;T from a small regional bank to  the tenth largest financial institution  in the country he has strong views ab